C(1,[2],6,[41,45],[[18,37],[133,166]],'On a un isom canonique','For smooth proper X/K over a complete discretely valued mixed-characteristic field with perfect residue field, the C_p-linear Hodge–Tate comparison decomposes H^n_et into H^b(X,Ω^a)⊗C_p(−a), a+b=n, functorially.','open on the pages',N,'B','Faltings1988, Chapter III Theorem4.1. The filtered de Rham splitting requested on041 is stronger than this graded statement.',{'publication':'1988','received':'1987-02-27','first_circulation':'not established'},'Established Hodge–Tate comparison; do not infer a canonical splitting of H_dR over K.',['Faltings1988','BrinonConrad2009'],'high',['R67-F01'])
C(2,[2,5],6,[45],[[154,166]],'compatible avec les produits; Z de codim p','The comparison is multiplicative and takes algebraic cycle classes to the expected Hodge component with its Tate twist.','open on the pages',N,'B','Faltings1988 comparison construction; BMS2018 Theorem1.1(i) supplies compatible filtered crystalline comparison in good reduction.',{'publication':'1988; 2018','first_circulation':'BMS announcement2015; fullpreprint2016'},'Established with the comparison’s geometric and coefficient hypotheses; separate Hodge–Tate from integral cycle comparison.',['Faltings1988','BMS2018'],'medium',['R67-F01','R67-F10'])
C(3,[3],6,[41],[[25,26]],'Tout Q_ell(pi)-module simple ... contenu dans un C(i) ?','Every finite-dimensional irreducible continuous Q_p-representation of G_K embeds in C_p(i) for some integer i.','open on the pages','Counterexample over Q_3: the continuous character ψ=exp((1/2)log〈χ_cyc〉) has Sen weight1/2. A nonzero map into C_p(i) would identify its C_p-extension with C_p(i), whose Sen weight is an integer.','B','False for arbitrary continuous representations; Sen’s operator gives the obstruction. BrinonConrad2009 §15.1, Theorem15.1.7 and Corollary15.1.14.',{'publication':'Sen1980/81; exposition2009','first_circulation':'not established'},'Refuted under the broad reconstructed quantifier; the geometric subclass is constrained by Hodge–Tate/de Rham conditions.',['BrinonConrad2009'],'high',['R67-F01'])
C(4,[3,7],6,[41,43],[[25,26],[71,103]],'M et M(i) ... tous deux contenus dans C ?','There can be an irreducible Q_p-representation M for which both M and a nonzero integer twist embed G_K-equivariantly in C_p.','open on the pages','Take an irreducible two-weight Hodge–Tate representation V with weights a≠b. Its two C_p-projections restrict to nonzero maps of V, hence injective Q_p-maps by irreducibility; twisting gives M and M(a−b) in C_p. Such V exist among irreducible two-dimensional crystalline representations of weights0,1.','B','BrinonConrad2009 §§8.3–8.4 and Hodge–Tate formalism §2.4 support the construction; this is an inference from those results, not a quotation.',{'publication':'2009 exposition','first_circulation':'not established'},'Positive examples exist. One must not argue from (C_p(i))^G=0 to the absence of higher-dimensional subrepresentations.',['BrinonConrad2009'],'medium',['R67-F01'])
C(5,[4,8],6,[41,43],[[28,32],[102,103]],'autres conditions; le plus petit groupe algébrique','The page seeks a classification of geometric p-adic representations and proposes the smallest algebraic subgroup containing a two-weight cocharacter.','open on the pages',N,'F','The unnamed Tate conjecture and the category of allowed geometric representations are not specified. Searches locate p-adic Hodge constraints, but no exact single theorem has these quantifiers.',{'publication':'not applicable'},'Identifiable as a programme, not as a determinate solved/open classification assertion.',['BrinonConrad2009','Faltings1988'],'high',['R67-F01'])
C(6,[6],6,[42],[[45,65]],'les applications ... sont continues','For a continuous action of a locally compact group on a locally compact Hausdorff E, the induced translations on C_c(E) with its stated inductive-limit topology and C_0(E) with the uniform norm are jointly continuous.','proof sketched','Support-control argument is given on the leaf. On C_0, compactly supported functions are dense; the translations are isometries, so strong continuity on the dense subspace implies joint continuity.','E','Classical continuous representation/compact-support argument. Exact Bourbaki source identity remains unidentified after phrase/title searches; no archive authorship attribution is made.',{'known_before':'classical functional analysis; exact textual publication not identified'},'The reconstructed theorem is standard. The missing topology definition and incomplete corollary cannot be certified from this leaf.',['BourbakiSpectral1967'],'medium',['R67-F02'])
C(7,[9],6,[44],[[107,129]],'Sp(f(x))=f(Sp(x)); (g∘f)(x)=g(f(x))','Holomorphic functional calculus in a unital complex Banach algebra has spectral mapping, the invertibility criterion and composition, for functions holomorphic on the required spectral neighbourhoods.','proof sketched','Spectral mapping is proved by factoring f(z)−lambda; composition uses Cauchy kernels and exchanging compact contour integrals. The missing integral sign at119 is necessary.','E','Classical holomorphic functional calculus; Bourbaki Théories spectrales provides the matching subject, but the exact draft leaf/proposition numbering has not been matched.',{'publication':'Bourbaki volume1967; results classical earlier'},'Established under the reconstructed Banach-algebra hypotheses; no identification of the typescript’s author or date.',['BourbakiSpectral1967'],'medium',['R67-F02'])
C(8,[10],6,[46],[[175,197]],'mesure ... formule de Cauchy; on a ∫ f dnu=0','Compact contour measures give the two Cauchy representations; vanishing of the Cauchy transform near K implies annihilation of functions holomorphic off K and vanishing at infinity, with disjoint compact supports justifying Fubini.','proof sketched','Choose finite piecewise-smooth contours around K inside the holomorphy region, use Banach-valued Cauchy formula, then Fubini on separated compact supports. The second lemma’s K/L inconsistency must be resolved before using its literal statement.','E','Classical Cauchy functional calculus; exact source bibliographic identity not recovered.',{'known_before':'classical Cauchy theory'},'Valid in the specified reconstructed setting. Literal second-lemma notation remains scan-dependent.',['BourbakiSpectral1967'],'medium',['R67-F02'])
C(9,[7],6,[43],[[71,99]],'V^10 ... invariants; V^01 ...','A Hodge–Tate C_p-representation supported in weights0 and1 is recovered as the sum of the scalar extensions of its two twisted invariant spaces; a chosen Q_p-lattice alone need not create this structure.','stated without proof',N,'E','Tate–Sen invariant calculation and modern Hodge–Tate formalism; BrinonConrad2009 §§2.2–2.4.',{'publication':'Tate1967; exposition2009','first_circulation':'not established'},'Established conditionally on the two-weight Hodge–Tate assumption. The unresolved implication sign should not be read universally.',['BrinonConrad2009'],'high',['R67-F01'])
C(10,[11],6,[48],[[209,215]],'hypersingulières i.e. sans pts d’ordre p','Over algebraically closed k of characteristic p, p-rank-zero abelian varieties with isogenous connected formal groups are isogenous.','open on the pages','Refutation for every p: Lenstra–Oort constructs geometrically simple A3 of type G21+G12 and A4 of type G21+G12+G11; take supersingular E. A4 and A3×E have dimension4,p-rank0 and isogenous formal groups, but are not isogenous since geometric simplicity is isogeny-invariant. All objects lie over Fbar_p.','B','LenstraOort1974 §2 pp48–51 supplies exactly the required types and geometric simplicity. The terminology is not modern supersingularity.',{'received':'1973-06-01','publication':'1974','first_circulation':'a May1973 report is indexed; receipt date verified on original'},'False as asked. Restricting to all slopes1/2 gives the familiar positive supersingular isogeny class.',['LenstraOort1974','Oort1974'],'high',['R67-F03'])
C(11,[11],6,[48],[[211,215]],'dimension1 ... Deuring; dimension2 ... produit de deux courbes elliptiques','An abelian surface of p-rank zero over an algebraically closed field is supersingular and isogenous to a product of supersingular elliptic curves.','open on the pages','For a height4 polarized isocrystal with no slopes0,1, non-half slopes a/b must appear with dual slope1−a/b, each with multiplicity divisible by b; b≥3 would need height≥6. Hence all slopes are1/2.','B','Oort’s supersingular theorem is cited in the opened LenstraOort1974 introduction; Oort1974 publication metadata verified, primary theorem text not separately obtained.',{'publication':'1974','first_circulation':'Oort Aarhus preprint1972/73 no44 cited by Lenstra–Oort'},'Established in every characteristic p. The manuscript’s report of Manin for p=2 is a historical partial state, not the modern bound.',['LenstraOort1974','Oort1974'],'medium',['R67-F03'])
C(12,[12,13],6,[48,49],[[217,229]],'famille de dimension2; il y a des simples','Simple p-rank-one abelian surfaces exist with formal type G10+G11, hence their formal group can be isogenous to that of an ordinary elliptic times a supersingular elliptic product.','proof sketched','Lenstra–Oort parameters t=1,(n,m)=(1,0),h=1 give a geometrically simple surface. This checks existence independently of the manuscript’s moduli-dimension argument.','B','LenstraOort1974 §2, same construction. Dimension/purity claims about the moduli locus are separate and not proved by that theorem.',{'publication':'1974','received':'1973-06-01'},'Existence established. The claim that the nonisomorphic-product complement is exactly the simple locus is false.',['LenstraOort1974'],'high',['R67-F03'])
C(13,[12],6,[49],[[223,225]],'isogène ... isomorphe à un produit E′×F′','A surface isogenous to an ordinary elliptic curve times a supersingular elliptic curve is necessarily isomorphic, as an unpolarized abelian variety, to a product of elliptic curves.','false as stated','Over Fbar_7 use J(C), C:y²=(x²−1)(x²−2)(x²−5). C is smooth genus2; quotient elliptic curves have8 and12 F7-points, traces0 and−4. Degree-two quotient maps give a (2,2)-isogeny. The two factors are nonisogenous (different p-ranks); every principal polarization on a product of such factors is a product polarization, whereas the Jacobian theta divisor is the irreducible curve C. Thus J(C) is not a product. See proof in computation_notes.md and exact code output.','B','Refuted independently here; LenstraOort1974 confirms the wider distinction between formal splitting and abelian splitting.',{'refutation_in_this_run':'2026-11-19'},'False under the natural abelian-surface reading of the unsure phrase tte courbe. The original isogène version is not the disputed sharpening.',['LenstraOort1974'],'high',['R67-F03'])
C(14,[12],6,[49],[[225,229]],'famille discrète; définies sur une extension finie de k','For the mixed ordinary/supersingular surface type, isogenous varieties are claimed to descend to finite extensions of a field of definition; examples are requested over fields of transcendence degrees0,1,2.','stated without proof',N,'C','LenstraOort1974 supplies finite-field simple examples, but not this exact descent assertion. The manuscript’s argument by the one-dimensional product locus does not prove the claim for all isogenous surfaces.',{'publication':'1974 partial existence result'},'Finite-field existence settled; exact descent/dimension formulation not certified. This is not a claim that general isogeny leaves are discrete.',['LenstraOort1974'],'low',['R67-F03'])
C(15,[14],6,[50,51],[[233,241]],'tordre ... par n’importe quelle unité theta; det ... transcendant','There exist height-two one-dimensional formal p-divisible groups over a finite field which cannot embed into the formal group of any abelian variety after any finite extension.','proof sketched','Take a supersingular elliptic formal group over a square finite field where Frobenius is scalar. Twist by a central u∈1+pZ_p transcendental over Q. Its continuous procyclic descent cocycle is u^z. Twisted Frobenius has eigenvalue u times an algebraic nonzero scalar; all positive powers remain transcendental. An embedding into an abelian variety would force an algebraic Frobenius eigenvalue, contradiction. For p=2 take u∈1+4Z_2.','C','Independent reconstruction proves the finite-field/finite-extension clause. Searches of the exact formal-twist phrasing, Honda–Tate and bibliographies did not identify a printed source for this exact example.',{'checked_in_this_run':'2026-11-19','first_publication':'not identified'},'The restricted construction is valid; the author’s arbitrary-unit and finitely-generated-extension generality needs separate descent checks.',['LenstraOort1974','Pink2005'],'medium',['R67-F03'])
C(16,[14],6,[50],[[235,235]],'sur un corps ... extension de type fini','The nonembedding of the twisted formal group persists after every finitely generated extension of its finite field.','stated without proof',N,'C','The finite-extension obstruction is clear, but the transition to transcendental finitely generated fields requires spreading out and specialization of the embedding. No exact published theorem was matched in this run.',{'first_publication':'not identified'},'Residual proof obligation: extend finite truncations compatibly and specialize to a closed point without losing injectivity. Not declared an open problem in the modern literature.',['LenstraOort1974'],'low',['R67-F03'])
C(17,[15],6,[53,54],[[254,318]],'T_p(M_0)^vee⊗N_0; un tore formel','Ordinary p-divisible-group deformations are extensions of their unique lifted étale part by their multiplicative part, parametrized by a formal torus; Serre–Tate identifies these with ordinary abelian deformations.','proof sketched',N,'B','Katz1981 Theorem2.1 and its deformation pairing give the precise ordinary statement.',{'publication':'1981','first_circulation':'Serre–Tate result earlier; exact original circulation not established here'},'Established for ordinary deformations over local Artin bases with the specified residue field and framing.',['Katz1981'],'high',['R67-F04'])
C(18,[15],6,[54],[[310,318]],'torseur ... sur le schéma modulaire de N_0','Allowing a nonmultiplicative connected N_0 to deform gives a torsor under the associated formal p-divisible group over its deformation base.','stated without proof',N,'C','Katz1981 verifies the ordinary special case. Searches of extension deformation, connected–étale deformation and Grothendieck–Messing theory did not identify this precise torsor statement with all base hypotheses.',{'publication':'ordinary case1981 exposition; exact general formulation not identified'},'Keep as a precise extension-deformation lead; do not claim that ordinary Serre–Tate theory proves every connected-part case.',['Katz1981','BMS2018'],'low',['R67-F04'])
C(19,[16,17],6,[55,56],[[322,375]],'v_*xi=u^*xi′; alpha−beta','For fixed maps on the ends of two extensions in an abelian category, a map of extensions exists iff pushout and pullback classes agree. Its choices form a torsor under Hom(M,N′); in the rigid infinitesimal p-divisible setting the fixed-special-fibre map is unique. The deformation locus is the kernel of the induced difference map.','proof sketched','Yoneda extension criterion: pull back E′ along u and push out E along v; a map exists exactly when the resulting extensions are isomorphic. Automorphisms fixing ends are Hom(M,N′).','E','The extension-class criterion is formal homological algebra; Katz1981 Theorem2.1(4) gives the ordinary lifting criterion in coordinates.',{'known_before':'Yoneda extension formalism','publication':'1981 exact ordinary formulation'},'Established with uniqueness restricted to the rigid framed deformation problem.',['Katz1981'],'high',['R67-F04'])
C(20,[18],6,[56,57],[[365,404]],'intersection complète; Gorenstein; irréductible; réduit lisse','At an ordinary polarized deformation point, the formal multiplicative-type kernel is a complete intersection, Cohen–Macaulay and Gorenstein, with a smooth reduced ring and local irreducibility; its dimension is g(g+1)/2, and prime-to-p polarization makes it smooth.','proof sketched','Smith form over Z_p reduces formal-torus character equations to powers z_j^(p^e_j) in characteristic p. They form a regular sequence. The reduced quotient is a power-series ring; symmetry for a polarization leaves g(g+1)/2 free parameters. The code records this model and dimension values, not a general moduli proof.','B','Katz1981 deformation pairing and morphism criterion; this run derives the local multiplicative-kernel consequences. Coarse moduli quotients and the cancelled mixed-characteristic flatness doubt are excluded.',{'publication':'1981 exposition','checked_in_this_run':'2026-11-19'},'Valid in the framed ordinary deformation/fine moduli setting; do not extend to every polarized moduli singularity.',['Katz1981'],'medium',['R67-F04'])
C(21,[19],6,[59],[[412,423]],'Ext^1(G_a,G_a) ... libre à gauche ... W_2','Commutative fppf extensions of G_a by G_a over characteristic-p A have the stated Frobenius-skew module structure generated by the Witt extension.','proof sketched','The base-field reduction and action by F^i are stated, not fully developed. The skew identity F[a]=[a^p]F is checked symbolically; it does not by itself prove freeness for arbitrary A.','C','The manuscript cites DemazureGabriel1970 II§3,4.6. Bibliographic identity was verified, but that exact paragraph was not obtained; Pink2005 §§18–23 verifies adjacent Witt/Dieudonné structure only over perfect fields.',{'publication':'1970 cited book;2005 checked exposition'},'Do not certify arbitrary-base sheaf/global Ext or left/right conventions from the nearby perfect-field theorem. Exact arbitrary-A identification remains a source-check residual.',['DemazureGabriel1970','Pink2005'],'low',['R67-F05'])
C(22,[19,20],6,[59],[[425,472]],'restriction ... iso; V ... nul; à droite','For H=G_a or a finite locally free subgroup as specified, the restriction Ext^1(H,W_n)→Ext^1(H,W_1), V-transition vanishing, restriction from G_a, and right-skew generation by W_(n+1) are claimed.','proof sketched','The n=1 argument and induction via0→G_a→W_n→W_(n−1)→0 are present. Part a_2 has an unspecified arrow; left/right actions refer to different Ext variables.','C','DemazureGabriel1970 V§1,2.2 is the author’s exact lead. Pink2005 Proposition22.2 proves a related finite-Witt transition vanishing over perfect fields, not the full arbitrary-base four-part assertion.',{'publication':'1970 cited;2005 checked limited result'},'Partly matched; keep the exact arrow, topology and Ext-variance residuals. The false implication “similar Witt theorem therefore all parts proved” is avoided.',['DemazureGabriel1970','Pink2005'],'low',['R67-F05'])
C(23,[21,22],6,[60],[[476,500]],'Ext^1(H,W)=0; Non déjà alpha_p','The author asserts sheaf-Ext vanishing into colim_V W_n for the indicated unipotent groups, but immediately rejects its extrapolation to global Ext on an affine imperfect field.','corrected by himself later: global question and Non both on060 lines482–485','The distinction is local-to-global Ext. H^1_fppf(k,alpha_p)=k/k^p is a useful diagnostic, not asserted equal to the displayed Ext group. Pink’s transition calculation applies to perfect-field finite Witt kernels.','C','Perfect-field vanishing is supported by Pink2005 Proposition22.2 and dévissage. Exact imperfect-field global counterexample and sheaf variant need the original Witt direct-system convention; searches did not identify it.',{'publication':'2005 explicit proof of limited transition result'},'The global conjecture is withdrawn on the page. The live imperfect-field sheaf assertion is not promoted to a certified theorem.',['Pink2005','DemazureGabriel1970'],'medium',['R67-F05'])
C(24,[22],6,[60],[[500,531]],'Hom(G,W_n) lisse ...; FV=VF=p','The deleted part claims Hom(G,W_n) is a smooth affine space of the indicated dimension and begins Dieudonné relations Fλ=λ^pF, λV=Vλ^p,FV=VF=p.','corrected by himself later: entire part(a) and§5 cancelled on060','Frobenius-skew relation is checked. The deleted dimension r=dim G/G^0 is ambiguous and must not be turned into a live representability result.','F','Pink2005 §21 verifies Witt relations in its perfect-field setting, but does not identify the cancelled Hom dimension statement.','No fate date for a cancelled underdefined assertion','Keep the algebraic relations as historical cancelled background; do not resurrect the smoothness/dimension claim.',['Pink2005'],'high',['R67-F05'])
C(25,[23],6,[61],[[539,594]],'s’étend de façon unique; anti-équivalence ... vérifier','Finite étale p-primary groups lift uniquely through nilpotent thickenings. Finite locally free unit-root Frobenius modules encode fixed-level étale local systems; mixed torsion lengths require the corresponding locally presented torsion category.','proof sketched; anti-equivalence self-flagged vérifier',N,'B','SGA1 I§8 gives nilpotent étale invariance. BhattScholze2023 Proposition3.4 proves the F_p/perfect-complex equivalence, citing Katz1973 Proposition4.1.1 for vector bundles.','1960/61 seminar; SGA1 publication1971; Katz1973; later2023','Established in precisely specified categories; not every smooth Witt-module scheme is an étale local system.',['SGA1','BhattScholze2023','Pink2005'],'high',['R67-F06'])
C(26,[24],6,[62],[[597,615]],'RHom_Z(G,O_S) ≃ check G⊗^L O_S','The unshifted derived Hom/derived tensor equality is valid as printed under standard cohomological conventions.','false as stated under standard cohomological indexing','For G=Z/p, O=F_p: RHom has nonzero H^0,H^1, whereas derived tensor has nonzero H^(−1),H^0. Corrected formula uses tensor[−1] when check G=Hom(G,Q_p/Z_p). The two-term resolution is recorded in code/output.','E','This is the elementary perfect-duality shift from the resolution[Z→p Z]; no later theory is needed to diagnose the displayed mismatch.','checked2026-11-19','Reading/convention alert, not an editorial correction. Establish the author’s grading before changing the ink.',['Pink2005'],'high',['R67-F06'])
C(27,[25],6,[62,63],[[619,682]],'V est un isomorphisme; F=pV^(−1)','Cartier duality interchanges finite étale and multiplicative-type p-groups, and reverses F,V. A map from connected multiplicative p-groups to finite étale groups is zero; the corresponding crystal map vanishes by invertible/nilpotent iteration.','proof sketched','A finite iteration gives f=V^(−n)fV^n=0 (or F dual). The converse group direction fails over F_p[epsilon]/epsilon^p:1↦1+epsilon defines nonzero Z/p→mu_p.','E','Pink2005 §§14–15,26–28 prove the perfect-field finite-group form; the connected-to-étale zero argument works by fibrewise connectedness more generally.','classical Cartier/Dieudonné theory; checked exposition2005','Valid in the stated direction and categories. Empty part(b) does not authorize a symmetric vanishing claim.',['Pink2005'],'high',['R67-F06'])
C(28,[26,28],6,[65,66],[[688,755]],'Le foncteur phi est une équivalence','Finite-rank étale F_p-local systems on a characteristic-p scheme are equivalent to vector bundles with a Frobenius-linear isomorphism.','proof sketched','Locally the fixed-point algebra is R[x_i]/(x_i^p−Σ(A^(−1))_ij x_j). Monic relatively prime leading monomials give rank p^r; the Jacobian is invertible. Over an algebraic closure the fixed vectors span, yielding the inverse by étale descent. Code checks a rank2,p3 instance; the proof is general.','B','BhattScholze2023 Proposition3.4; the text explicitly identifies Katz1973 Proposition4.1.1 as the vector-bundle antecedent.','Katz1973; preprint2021/publication2023 for the opened modern source','Established without the normality assumption sometimes imposed in the older reference.',['BhattScholze2023'],'high',['R67-F06'])
C(29,[27],6,[66],[[762,792]],'Zar ou fppf ... kif kif','The final diagram extends the equivalence to Witt modules locally sums of W_n and a category described merely as smooth W-module schemes.','stated without proof',N,'F','Pink2005 §27 and BrinonConrad2009 §3 give precise torsion/unit-F categories; the last manuscript category omits enough conditions that its exact equivalence is not identifiable.','modern precise formulations2005,2009','Do not certify the literal broad category. Bounded length, local presentation and Frobenius structure must be specified.',['Pink2005','BrinonConrad2009'],'high',['R67-F06'])
C(30,[29,30],7,[3,4,5],[[833,919]],'pro-objet; deux structures','Principal-parts diagonal neighbourhoods form the cosimplicial pro-system with its multiple O_X-actions; adjoining a vertex gives the shifted augmentation; finite-order differential operators are represented through principal parts.','proof sketched','The tensor formula must insert the unit in the omitted factor, with indices matched to the cosimplicial map; the x_i/a_i mismatch is a reading issue.','A','Grothendieck1968 §§6.2–6.3; EGAIV4 §16.','1966 talks/1968 publication; EGA1967','Established principal-parts construction; pro and completed categories remain distinct.',['Grothendieck1968','EGAIV4','Fiorot2011'],'high',['R67-F07'])
C(31,[32,33],7,[6,7,8,59],[[921,960],[1934,1960]],'formalisation; sections stationnaires','Formalization linearizes finite-order differential operators into stratified pro-maps, with horizontal/stationary sections recovering the original module, in the coherent smooth setting.','proof sketched','The sample[d/dt,t]=1 checks differential order and failure of right O_X-linearity. Global equivalence requires the pro-category constructions.','B','Fiorot2011 §§2–3, Theorems5.6 and6.2 give a precise bounded coherent pro formulation.','arXiv2007-10-02; publication2011','Established for smooth separated noetherian finite-type C-schemes with the specified coherent pro categories. The manuscript’s arbitrary quasi-coherent scope is not silently included.',['Fiorot2011','Grothendieck1968'],'high',['R67-F07'])
C(32,[34],7,[9],[[957,960]],'A.R.-pro-Modules ... catégorie abélienne ?','The intended Artin–Rees pro-module category is abelian, with no quasi-coherence assumption needed.','open on the pages',N,'C','Pro(Coh(X)) is abelian in Fiorot2011 §2 under its noetherian hypotheses. This does not identify the manuscript’s underdefined AR quotient or prove its non-quasi-coherent extension.','2007 preprint/2011 publication','Precise coherent version settled; exact unrestricted question remains a formulation issue, not a claimed modern open theorem.',['Fiorot2011','Liu2025'],'medium',['R67-F07'])
C(33,[35,36],7,[9],[[957,960]],'résolution simpliciale','The augmented shifted diagonal complex is contractible after forgetting the stratification, and its inverse limit is exact under the asserted coefficient conditions.','proof sketched','The extra degeneracy inserting the distinguished vertex gives dh+hd=id. Limit exactness is an additional statement, not implied by this identity unless the contraction is compatible at the required level.','A','Grothendieck1968 §§6.3–6.4; Fiorot2011 Theorem2.10 supplies induced pro-object acyclicity.','talks1966; publication1968; modern precise pro account2007/2011','The standard coherent pro construction works; unqualified exactness of inverse limits is not asserted.',['Grothendieck1968','Fiorot2011','Liu2025'],'medium',['R67-F07'])
C(34,[37,39,40],7,[10,11,13],[[962,1009],[1027,1057]],'E_2^{p,q}=H_str^p(V^q)','A bounded-below differential complex yields the formalization hypercohomology comparison and its spectral sequence when the pro-limit and flatness assumptions hold.','proof sketched','Bicomplex total differential is d_h+(−1)^p d_v; the spectral sequence needs boundedness/convergence, and replacing Rlim by lim needs derived-limit vanishing.','A','Grothendieck1968 §6.4; Fiorot2011 Theorem5.6 proves bounded coherent derived equivalence.','talks1966; publication1968;2007/2011','Conditional theorem retained. No blanket passage from every quasi-coherent inverse system to its ordinary limit.',['Grothendieck1968','Fiorot2011','Liu2025'],'medium',['R67-F07'])
C(35,[38],7,[12],[[1011,1025]],'conditions de platitude infinitésimale','Flatness of X/S alone is proposed to ensure cohomology commutes with all diagonal neighbourhood pullbacks and induces a stratification.','open on the pages',N,'C','EGAIV4 §16 gives principal-parts smoothness/flatness criteria; Fiorot2011 assumes smoothness. No exact theorem covering the proposed arbitrary flat case was matched.','EGA1967; modern smooth case2011','Smooth finite-presentation case is safe. The general infinitesimal-flatness criterion remains to be formulated/verified.',['EGAIV4','Fiorot2011'],'medium',['R67-F07'])
C(36,[41],7,[14,15],[[1059,1096]],'lemme de Poincaré formel','For smooth X over a Q-scheme S, the linearized relative de Rham complex resolves the structure crystal, so infinitesimal cohomology agrees with de Rham hypercohomology.','proof sketched','Formal integration divides by positive integers; failure in characteristic p is certified by t^(p−1)dt. Hence a Q-base, not merely char(R)=0, is required.','A','Grothendieck1968 Theorem4.1 and§6.5; Liu2025 Theorem11.2 gives a precise completed/crystal version.','1966 talks/1968 publication; Liu2025-12-02 preprint','Established with the stated smoothness and Q-base.',['Grothendieck1968','Liu2025'],'high',['R67-F07'])
C(37,[43],7,[16,17],[[1098,1135]],'C’est en effet faux; i>dim X','The non-PD stratification/infinitesimal cohomology of an ordinary abelian scheme over the indicated Artin base vanishes in degrees above its dimension; thus it cannot be the desired Weil cohomology.','corrected by himself later: hope on016–017, explicit negative statement on017','The author uses exactness of invariants for a formal multiplicative group to reduce to H^i(X,O_X), which vanishes for i>dim X. The top de Rham group of a positive-dimensional abelian variety is nonzero.','A','Grothendieck1968 §6.5, pp350–351, retains this example.','1966 talks/1968 publication','Correct for the draft non-PD site. False if one replaces that object by modern crystalline cohomology.',['Grothendieck1968','Liu2025'],'high',['R67-F07'])
C(38,[45],7,[18],[[1137,1165]],'lim ... tensor Q ...','An inverse limit cannot in general be interchanged with inverting p.','stated without proof','For the system Z/p^n with surjective transition maps, lim=Z_p; tensoring its limit with Q_p gives Q_p, but each term tensorQ_p=0, whose limit is0.','E','Elementary algebra; also cautioned in BrinonConrad2009 §4.4 in the construction of B_dR.','known before; checked2026-11-19','Established obstruction; it explains why the site and coefficient order must be fixed.',['BrinonConrad2009'],'high',['R67-F08'])
C(39,[45,59,66],7,[18,19,38,44],[[1137,1173],[1509,1515],[1646,1656]],'site ... W.M.; topologie de Zariski','A weak-lifting site should globalize affine Monsky–Washnitzer cohomology and compare with proper formal de Rham cohomology.','open on the pages',N,'C','MonskyWashnitzer1964 announces formal cohomology sheaves; vanDerPut1986 §§2–4 treats the rational affine theory; Grothendieck1968 §7.5 adds PD data to a further site proposal. The exact draft topology is not fully fixed.','Princeton notes1963; PNAS1964; FormalCohomologyI1968','Rational weak/rigid theories realize the aim, but the exact integral site and universal proper comparison written here are not a single verified theorem.',['MonskyWashnitzer1964','MonskyWashnitzer1968','vanDerPut1986','Grothendieck1968'],'medium',['R67-F08'])
C(40,[46],7,[21,22,36],[[1175,1229],[1457,1471]],'Th ... isomorphisme','Algebraic de Rham hypercohomology of a smooth locally finite-type C-scheme agrees with analytic/singular cohomology; on a smooth affine, global algebraic forms compute it.','stated without proof; proof referred to mes papiers bleus',N,'A','Grothendieck1966 Theorems1 and1′; its first footnote dates the letter to Atiyah to14October1963.','circulated1963-10-14; received1965-07-05; published1966','Established. In these1966 lectures it is already the author’s theorem, not an unsolved conjecture.',['Grothendieck1966'],'high',['R67-F09'])
C(41,[46],7,[21],[[1182,1210]],'Poincaré; faisceaux fins; Stein','Smooth de Rham forms resolve constants and are fine; holomorphic forms resolve constants, and on Stein manifolds their coherent higher cohomology vanishes, so global holomorphic forms compute singular cohomology.','proof sketched',N,'E','Classical analytic de Rham/Cartan theorem; recapitulated in Grothendieck1968 §§1.1–1.2.','known before1966','Established with smooth manifold/Stein hypotheses; no analogous global-form assertion for every complex manifold.',['Grothendieck1968'],'high',['R67-F09'])
C(42,[47,49,42],7,[23],[[1246,1253]],'H_DR^0(X)≃H^0(X,O_X)^p','For a smooth scheme over a perfect characteristic-p field, the kernel of d on functions is the relative Frobenius image. For A^1, both H^0 andH^1 of the naive de Rham complex are infinite-dimensional.','stated without proof','d(t^n)=n t^(n−1)dt. Thus H^0=k[t^p], H^1=k[t^p]t^(p−1)dt. Over imperfect k, constants need not be literal pth powers: k=F_p(u) already diagnoses the missing perfectness.','E','Cartier theory; Katz1970 §5.1 treats the Frobenius/zero-p-curvature setting.','Cartier result before1966; opened exposition1970','Correct with perfectness or relative-Frobenius wording. Nonproperness alone does not force infinite dimension in every example.',['Katz1970'],'high',['R67-F09'])
C(43,[47],7,[23],[[1255,1263]],'dim H_DR^1(X)≥2dim Pic_X; >','For smooth proper X over a perfect field, dim H^1_dR(X) is at least twice dim Pic^0_red(X), and can be strictly larger; the source tentatively attributes the excess to p-torsion.','stated without proof',N,'C','BMS2018 Theorem1.1(ii) explains integral torsion inequalities in liftable good-reduction situations, but is not an exact theorem about every smooth proper nonliftable X or this precise Picard bound.','modern integral comparison2015 announcement/2018 publication','The broad inequality is compatible with crystalline rank/Picard theory; this run does not certify a universal exact torsion formula or an explicit strict Picard example from the page.',['BMS2018','Grothendieck1968'],'medium',['R67-F09','R67-F10'])
C(44,[47],7,[24],[[1265,1267]],'seulement le nb mod p','If a Lefschetz trace formula is evaluated in a characteristic-p coefficient field, it determines the integer fixed-point count only through its image modulo p.','stated without proof','Integers n and n+p have the same image in every characteristic-p field; the information loss is immediate.','E','The coefficient obstruction is algebraic; it is retained in Grothendieck1968 §1.5.','known before1966','Correct as an information-loss statement, not a proof that the naive de Rham theory satisfies a trace formula.',['Grothendieck1968'],'high',['R67-F09'])
C(45,[50],7,[24],[[1269,1273]],'un point de rebr. ordinaire ... pas ... premier nb de Betti','The full Kähler–de Rham complex of the ordinary affine cusp y²=x³ over C has wrong first Betti number.','false as stated under the indicated reading','A is positively graded with weights2,3. Euler E=2x∂x+3y∂y preserves the relation. On weight w>0, Cartan gives h=i_E/w and dh+hd=1; hence H^i(Ω_A^bullet)=0 for i>0. Analytic normalization C→C^an is a homeomorphism, so H^1_sing=0. Ω²=A/(x²,y)dx∧dy is nonzero; d(3y dx−2xdy)=−5dx∧dy.','B','Independent refutation here; ArapuraKang2011 §4 confirms the importance of Ω² and supplies genuine failure examples. MO128853 records the same false-cusp pitfall and corrections, used only as a lead.','ArapuraKang received2009-10-16/published2011; independentcheck2026-11-19','Specific cusp example fails. The general singular-variety criticism remains valid; see refinedP67-C046.',['ArapuraKang2011','MO128853'],'high',['R67-F09'])
C(46,[50,51],7,[24],[[1269,1275]],'Le point de vue De Rham classique ... singulières','For singular complex varieties, naive Kähler–de Rham hypercohomology need not agree with singular cohomology.','stated without proof; proposed ordinary-cusp example invalid','Arapura–Kang’s affine plane curve x^5+y^5+x²y²=0 has isolated origin singularity with μ=11,τ=10; their Proposition4.2 gives excess H^1. This example is a source result, not a Gröbner computation independently certified here.','B','ArapuraKang2011 Proposition4.2 and Example4.4 give an exact replacement; Hartshorne1975 uses completed ambient de Rham cohomology.','received2009/published2011; Hartshorne1975','Correct general claim; use the full exterior complex and a genuine non-quasihomogeneous example.',['ArapuraKang2011','Hartshorne1975'],'high',['R67-F09'])
C(47,[51,52],7,[25,26,27],[[1276,1331]],'bonne catégorie; f_!,f^!; suites spectrales','Construct an algebraic coefficient/operations formalism encompassing coherent and de Rham-type complexes, including Leray, duality, exceptional operations and homology.','open on the pages',N,'C','KatzOda1968 §4 supplies a relative de Rham Leray sequence for smooth morphisms with affine base. Fiorot2011 Theorem5.6 gives the pro differential/stratified equivalence. These are precise portions, not a single universal category covering every requested operation and singular case.','Katz–Oda received1968-04-19/published1968; Fiorot2007/2011','The programme was developed in several coefficient theories. Exact unrestricted formulation is not identified; no generic D-module reference is treated as proof of every clause.',['KatzOda1968','Fiorot2011','Hartshorne1975'],'medium',['R67-F07','R67-F09'])
C(48,[53],7,[28,29,30],[[1333,1370]],'isom can ... Z_l; coefficients entiers indépendants de l','For finite-type complex algebraic X, comparison with finitely generated integral singular cohomology gives l-independent Betti ranks, finite-prime torsion, additive reconstruction from all l-adic groups, and integral l-independent endomorphism polynomials.','proof sketched','A finitely generated abelian group Z^r⊕T is recovered by r and all finite l-primary summands T_l. An endomorphism of its free quotient has a single integral matrix, hence one characteristic polynomial over all Z_l.','A','SGA4 XVI comparison theorem; SGA1 XII provides the analytic comparison framework. The finite-generation algebra is independently checked here.','SGA4 seminars1963/64; publication1973','Established with finite type and coefficient-completion hypotheses. Abstract additive groups do not determine ring structure.',['SGA4','SGA1'],'high',['R67-F11'])
C(49,[54],7,[30],[[1371,1376]],'il semble qu’il faille la résolution des singularités','General finite-dimensionality of l-adic cohomology in positive characteristic is open unless one has resolution.','open on the pages','No computation; the proposed necessity of resolution is a historical assessment.','A','SGA4 finiteness theorems establish constructibility/finiteness for finite-type schemes with torsion coefficients invertible on the base; l-adic finiteness follows under its standard hypotheses.','seminar1963/64; publication1972–73','General finiteness is established. Resolution is not a necessary prerequisite in the form feared here.',['SGA4'],'medium',['R67-F11'])
C(50,[54],7,[30,31],[[1376,1391]],'on ignore ... rang H^2; polynômes caractéristiques','For smooth projective varieties over an algebraically closed characteristic-p field, the l-adic Betti numbers and characteristic polynomials of algebraic endomorphisms should be independent of l≠p and integral.','open on the pages',N,'B','KatzMessing1974 Theorems1,2 and the final corollary use Deligne’s purity to construct algebraic Künneth projectors over finite fields; spreading out transfers the degreewise statement to the stated setting.','Deligne lecturesJuly1973; Katz–Messing received1973-08-08/published1974','Established in this smooth projective setting. Do not extrapolate to arbitrary singular/nonproper cohomology or undefined correspondences.',['KatzMessing1974','Deligne1974'],'high',['R67-F11'])
C(51,[54],7,[31],[[1380,1391]],'de Z[1/p]','A rational coefficient integral at all primes l≠p lies in Z[1/p]; p-adic integrality is the remaining denominator condition.','proof sketched','Write q=a/b in lowest terms. For every prime l|b, v_l(q)<0; hence b is a p-power if q is l-integral for all l≠p.','E','Elementary valuation argument; KatzMessing1974 supplies the later crystalline side for Weil cohomology.','known before; relevant publication1974','Correct logical separation of rationality, l-integrality and p-integrality.',['KatzMessing1974'],'high',['R67-F11'])
C(52,[55],7,[32],[[1393,1406]],'argument de Serre ... Z_p ou Q_p','No Q_p-valued Weil cohomology with two-dimensional H^1 and faithful functorial endomorphism action exists for all characteristic-p varieties.','stated without proof','For supersingular E over Fbar_p, D=End^0(E)⊗Q_p is the quaternion division algebra of dimension4. Faithfulness embeds D into M_2(Q_p), also dimension4, forcing an isomorphism, impossible because M_2 has nontrivial zero divisors. This checks the reconstructed axioms explicitly.','E','The argument is reproduced in Grothendieck1968 §1.6; LenstraOort1974 uses the same Honda–Tate endomorphism framework.','attributed to Serre before1966; publication1968','Valid obstruction for the reconstructed Weil-type functor. It does not prohibit slope-zero Q_p cohomology or coefficients W(k)[1/p].',['Grothendieck1968','LenstraOort1974'],'high',['R67-F11'])
C(53,[55,59],7,[33,35,37],[[1408,1425],[1445,1455],[1492,1507]],'W(k); module de t.f.; ne dépend pas du relèvement','Witt-vector coefficients and crystalline cohomology give a finite integral invariant for smooth proper X_0/k and identify it with completed de Rham cohomology of a smooth formal W(k)-lift.','open on the pages','Proper coherent cohomology finiteness and the bounded relative de Rham complex give finite W-modules for a given proper lift; invariance requires a comparison theory.','B','Berthelot’s crystalline theory provides the construction; BMS2018 introduction and Theorem1.1 state the modern proper-smooth comparison setting.','Berthelot announcements1969/book1974; BMS2015/2018','Established for smooth proper schemes over perfect k. This is not integral finite generation for every nonproper weak lift.',['Berthelot1974','BMS2018'],'high',['R67-F08','R67-F10'])
C(54,[57],7,[34],[[1427,1434]],'Artin ... n+1','For noetherian X of characteristic p and Krull dimension n, constant finite p-primary étale coefficients have no cohomology above n+1.','stated without proof','Artin–Schreier0→F_p→O→F−1 O→0 and H^i_Zar(X,O)=0 for i>n give the bound; induction on the p-power exponent handles Z/p^r. Flat and étale cohomology agree for these finite étale coefficients.','E','SGA4 characteristic-p cohomological-dimension results; the displayed constant-coefficient bound also follows by the elementary sheaf argument just given.','Artin attribution before1966; SGA4 publication1972–73','Correct under the noetherian finite-dimensional hypotheses; no assertion for every finite flat coefficient.',['SGA4','Pink2005'],'high',['R67-F11'])
C(55,[57],7,[33,34,35],[[1415,1455]],'Raynaud ... dualité ... théorie du corps de classes','A dimension-one flat-cohomological duality/class-field construction should recover or compare with the proposed integral p-adic theory.','open on the pages',N,'F','The exact groups, dual objects, compact supports and base-field hypotheses are not stated. Searched flat duality, Raynaud, p-primary curves and crystalline comparison; no single exact assertion can be extracted.','not identifiable','Historical programme and attribution retained. No claim that it is a still-open modern theorem.',['BMS2018','Grothendieck1968'],'medium',['R67-F10','R67-F11'])
C(56,[60],7,[36,48],[[1472,1490],[1684,1727]],'Lg^*Rf_*(Ω)≃Rf′_*(Ω′)','For smooth quasi-compact quasi-separated f, relative de Rham derived direct image is compatible with derived base change; properness/local noetherian hypotheses give coherence.','proof sketched','Relative differential terms are flat over the base; a Čech resolution on an affine cover computes the derived pullback under the qcqs hypotheses. Ordinary cohomology base change still needs Tor control.','A','Grothendieck1968 §1.3, p309, explicitly distinguishes derived and ordinary compatibility.','1966 talks/1968 publication','Established with the relevant derived category and qcqs hypotheses; finite relative dimension is used for bounded de Rham complexes.',['Grothendieck1968','KatzOda1968'],'high',['R67-F12'])
C(57,[60],7,[48],[[1720,1727]],'cela montre trivialement ... g^*R^if_*','Derived base change alone implies ordinary cohomology base change along every section.','false as an inference without cohomological flatness','Perfect-complex countermodel over Z:K=[Z→p Z] in degrees0,1 has H^0=0 but H^0(K⊗^L F_p)=F_p. This refutes the implication, not every geometric instance. Proper smooth characteristic-zero de Rham cohomology is locally free, which repairs the intended application.','A','Grothendieck1968 §1.3 p309 supplies the explicit published qualification.','1968 published qualification; independentcheck2026-11-19','Add local freeness/Tor vanishing before using the ordinary pullback formula; see refinedP67-C056.',['Grothendieck1968'],'high',['R67-F12'])
C(58,[62],7,[39],[[1517,1540]],'unique à isom(non unique)près','A smooth affine k-scheme has a smooth formal lift to W(k), unique up to a nonunique isomorphism compatible with the special fibre.','stated without proof; SGA1960/61 III cited',N,'A','SGA1 III§7 gives formal smooth liftings; local obstruction classes lie in coherent cohomology, which vanishes on affine schemes.','seminar1960/61; book1971','Established for the formal lift. This does not provide a canonical isomorphism of lifts or of every nonformal algebraization.',['SGA1'],'high',['R67-F08'])
C(59,[62],7,[40],[[1542,1575]],'H^1 ... de rang infini sur W','The continuous de Rham H^1 of W{t} has infinite rank over W.','proof sketched','For disjoint infinite J_m={2^m(2r+1):r≥1}, set f_m=Σ_{j∈J_m}p^floor(j/2)t^(p^j−1). Each is restricted. In a finite nonzero K-linear combination, primitive coefficients along one support have valuation v(c_m)+floor(j/2)−j→−infinity. No such combination is a derivative in K{t}. Thus infinitely many independent classes survive after inverting p.','A','Grothendieck1968 §2.1 contains the same obstruction; this run supplies an independent infinite-family proof, stronger than testing one series.','1966 talks/1968 publication; independentproof2026-11-19','True infinite-rank assertion; code supplies finite exact samples and the proof handles the infinite quantifier.',['Grothendieck1968','vanDerPut1986'],'high',['R67-F08'])
C(60,[62],7,[40],[[1549,1575]],'v_p(a_n)−v_p(n+1)→+infty','A restricted integral form Σa_n t^n dt has an integral restricted primitive iff the valuation difference tends to+infinity.','false as an integral iff without coefficientwise integrality','The polynomial t^(p−1)dt passes every tail condition, but its unique nonconstant primitive t^p/p is not integral. Correct criterion also requires v_p(a_n)≥v_p(n+1) for every n; over K the tail criterion is sufficient.','E','Elementary termwise calculation; compare vanDerPut1986 §2 continuous differentials.','independentcheck2026-11-19','Use the refined criterion when working over W. The manuscript may intend the rationalized calculation, which the scans must clarify.',['vanDerPut1986'],'high',['R67-F08'])
C(61,[63],7,[41],[[1577,1590]],'|a_n|=O(1)rho^n,rho>1','The printed positive-exponent bound expresses overconvergent decay.','false as a literal condition','a_n=1 satisfies1≤rho^n but does not tend to0; even a_n=p^floor(sqrt(n)) is restricted and satisfies it but has no positive linear valuation lower bound. Decay needs rho^(−n), or0<rho<1 with the written sign.','E','vanDerPut1986 §2 defines weak completion/overconvergent convergence precisely.','independentcheck2026-11-19','Undecided ink; the formula cannot be used literally as the prose definition.',['vanDerPut1986'],'high',['R67-F08'])
C(62,[64],7,[41,42],[[1588,1601]],'idéaux ... automatiquement fermés','Every ideal of the intended Monsky–Washnitzer algebra is closed in the intended topology.','open on the pages',N,'C','vanDerPut1986 §2 gives noetherian weak completions and ideal/faithful-flat-completion statements. Closedness must be interpreted in the induced adic topology, not an unstated alternative topology.','MonskyWashnitzer1968; checked exposition1986','Settled in the standard dagger/adic setup; the page’s unstated topology is a real formulation dependency.',['vanDerPut1986','MonskyWashnitzer1968'],'medium',['R67-F08'])
C(63,[65],7,[42],[[1603,1629]],'relèvement plat ... W.M.; Omega^1 ... propriété universelle','Smooth affine residue algebras have smooth weak lifts; continuous Kähler differentials represent continuous derivations into finite modules and their exterior powers form the continuous de Rham complex.','open on the pages',N,'B','vanDerPut1986 §2.4 (notably2.4.4) and ErtlSprang2018 introduction give smooth weak lifts and uniqueness, tracing existence to Elkik.','Elkik1973; vanDerPut1986; ErtlSprang2017/2018','Established in the standard weak-completion category. The missing Ω^1 in the wedge formula and repeated A′ in the arrow are reading alerts.',['vanDerPut1986','ErtlSprang2018'],'high',['R67-F08'])
C(64,[65],7,[42,43],[[1624,1644]],'ne dépend que de sa réduction modulo p','For smooth affine k-schemes over perfect k, maps of weak lifts inducing the same special-fibre map induce the same integral Monsky–Washnitzer cohomology map; the groups are canonically lift-independent.','open on the pages','This is a cochain-homotopy theorem; mere nonunique isomorphism of the weak lifts does not establish it.','B','ErtlSprang2018 Proposition1, Proposition2 and Lemma3 give exact integral functoriality and independence in all degrees.','firstarXiv2017-09-21; publication2018','Established integrally for smooth affines. Canonical invariance does not imply integral finite generation.',['ErtlSprang2018'],'high',['R67-F08'])
C(65,[67,68],7,[45],[[1658,1664]],'Est-ce la torsion ... fibre générique ?','Integral crystalline/de Rham torsion of a smooth proper lift equals the p-primary étale torsion of its geometric generic fibre.','open on the pages','BMS2018 Theorem2.1 constructs a smooth projective relative surface over Z_2 with all geometric generic étale groups2-torsion-free but H^2_crys torsionF_2. This is an opened published counterexample, not independently constructed by this run.','B','BMS2018 Theorem1.1(ii) gives length inequalities; Theorem2.1 shows strict inequality.','announcement2015; fullpreprint2016-02-09; publication2018','Equality is false in general. In small-ramification/cohomological ranges stronger integral comparisons exist; no universal equality may be inferred from rational comparison.',['BMS2018'],'high',['R67-F10'])
C(66,[69],7,[46,47],[[1666,1682]],'H^i_WM ... modules de t.f. sur W','Integral Monsky–Washnitzer cohomology of every smooth affine variety is finitely generated over W.','stated without proof as expected property','On A^1, [t^(p^r−1)dt] has exact orderp^r: its primitive is t^(p^r)/p^r, and no lower p-power gives integral coefficients. Unbounded torsion exponents contradict finite generation over the DVR. Rational dagger H^1 vanishes, because exponential decay survives division by n+1.','B','ErtlShiho2020 introduction notes A^1 torsion; Theorem0.1 gives examples non-finitely generated even modulo torsion.','arXiv2018-12-10; publication2020','False integrally. Rational finite-dimensionality and proper crystalline finite generation are different true assertions.',['ErtlShiho2020','ErtlSprang2018'],'high',['R67-F08'])
C(67,[69],7,[46,47],[[1666,1682]],'Leray; Poincaré; Kunneth; Lefschetz–Weil','A rational p-adic theory should supply finite-dimensional groups, duality, Künneth, trace formulas and relative coefficients; the author reports these as missing or second-hand for W–M.','open on the pages',N,'B','vanDerPut1986 develops the rational affine theory and trace formulas; modern rigid cohomology realizes finiteness/duality with its precise support and overconvergence hypotheses. CaroDAddezio2025 is a caution about comparison with naive rational crystalline cohomology.','Monsky1968–1971; subsequent rigid theory;2025 comparison preprint','The rational programme is established in appropriate coefficient categories; the literal “hopeless” relative criticism is not a theorem. No claim of universal integral finiteness.',['vanDerPut1986','ErtlShiho2020','CaroDAddezio2025'],'medium',['R67-F08'])
C(68,[70,71,72],7,[48,49,50],[[1684,1756]],'isomorphismes canoniques ... transitifs; stratification','Canonical lift comparisons along infinitesimally close sections should arise from a transitive stratification, not just from derived base change.','open on the pages',N,'A','Grothendieck1968 §§3.1–3.4 formulate this mechanism and its caveats. KatzOda1968 supplies the connection; integral comparisons require the appropriate crystalline/convergence structure.','1966 talks/1968 publication','Valid organizing principle with stated stratification hypotheses; ordinary characteristic-p stratification is too strong.',['Grothendieck1968','KatzOda1968','Katz1970'],'high',['R67-F12'])
C(69,[73,74],7,[51],[[1758,1775]],'d i_D+i_Dd=Theta_D','Cartan’s formula makes a vertical Lie derivative nullhomotopic, so lifting base derivations yields a well-defined Gauss–Manin action on relative de Rham cohomology; the bracket identity gives integrability.','proof sketched','Independently recomputed on a general two-variable polynomial1-form for the indicated vector field. General proof checks on functions and their differentials and extends by the graded Leibniz rule; vertical-lift differences are Cartan homotopies.','E','KatzOda1968 §§2–3 constructs and explicitly identifies the connection. Cartan’s formula predates these pages.','Katz–Oda received1968-04-19/published1968','Established with smoothness needed to lift derivations locally. The manuscript’s tautological-looking opening H^1 formula remains unreadable.',['KatzOda1968'],'high',['R67-F12'])
C(70,[75],7,[52],[[1777,1788]],'connexion ... compatible avec chgt de base','For proper smooth characteristic-zero families, relative de Rham cohomology is locally free, has the integrable Gauss–Manin connection and commutes with base change; the connection matches its analytic counterpart.','stated without proof',N,'B','KatzOda1968 Theorem1/§2,§3 and Grothendieck1968 §§1.3,3 give the precise connection/base-change discussion.','1966/67 seminar;1968 publication','Established. The manuscript’s broader absolute-derived appendix is a promised construction, not a proof reproduced here.',['KatzOda1968','Grothendieck1968'],'high',['R67-F12'])
C(71,[72,75,76],7,[50,52,53],[[1745,1756],[1777,1808]],'espoir ... faux en car.p, famille de courbes elliptiques','The Gauss–Manin connection of every smooth proper characteristic-p family extends to an ordinary infinitesimal stratification.','corrected by himself later: explicit false on053 lines1790–1808','Over F_3[t,1/t(1−t)], E:y²=x(x−1)(x−t) is smooth proper after projective completion. In basis(ω,∇∂ω), the saved exact calculation yields ψ(∂)=1/(t²(t−1)²)[[t+1,1],[−t²+t−1,−t−1]], nonzero and square-zero. ∂³=0. Ordinary stratification would imply zero p-curvature, contradiction. Katz1984 p183 verifies the Picard–Fuchs equation; the cyclic-basis determinant is1/(2t(t−1)), a unit.','A','The withdrawal appears in Grothendieck1968 §3; Katz1970 §5 defines and analyzes p-curvature. The explicit p=3 certificate is this run’s independent test.','1966 correction/1968 publication;1970 theory;1984 explicit equation','False for ordinary stratification. Crystalline/PD structures are compatible with the nonzero nilpotent p-curvature and are not refuted.',['Grothendieck1968','Katz1970','Katz1984'],'high',['R67-F12'])
C(72,[77],7,[54,55],[[1810,1861]],'faisceaux spéciaux; stratification','On a formally smooth morphism, compatible crystal transition maps identify the local-lifting description with stratified modules; in characteristic zero integrable connections give the corresponding Taylor stratifications.','proof sketched',N,'A','Grothendieck1968 §§3.2–3.4,4.1–4.2; Fiorot2011 Theorem6.2 provides the coherent pro-crystal formulation.','1966 talks/1968 publication;2007/2011 pro formulation','Established with formal smoothness and the correct site; not an equivalence of every arbitrary integrable connection in characteristic p.',['Grothendieck1968','Fiorot2011','Liu2025'],'high',['R67-F07','R67-F12'])
C(73,[78,79],7,[56],[[1863,1876]],'Conj ... X ... pas lisse','For any locally finite-type C-scheme, infinitesimal cohomology of the structure crystal agrees canonically with singular cohomology of the analytification.','open on the pages',N,'B','Liu2025 Theorem1.1/14.1 proves exactly this statement. Theorem1.2 compares with ambient completed de Rham cohomology; its introduction reports an unpublished proof by Deligne via Hartshorne1971.','published conjecture1968; Deligne proof reported1971; current fullpreprintdated2025-12-02','Established in the opened2025 preprint, with earlier unpublished-proof attribution explicitly secondary to that source. Do not substitute naive singular Ω_X^bullet.',['Liu2025','Hartshorne1975','Grothendieck1968'],'high',['R67-F07','R67-F09'])
C(74,[80],7,[57,58],[[1878,1932]],'C^n(F)=lim ...; H_str=H(C^bullet)','The Čech–Alexander complex built from finite diagonal neighbourhoods computes infinitesimal/stratification cohomology for the quasi-coherent crystal systems satisfying the stated compatibility/limit conditions.','proof sketched','The n+1 projection pullbacks are canonically identified by the crystal. Evaluation on filtered representable approximants reverses colimit to inverse limit; exactness requires the stated coefficient condition.','A','Grothendieck1968 §§5.1,6.1; Liu2025 §§4–7 gives a precise treatment of the limit issues.','1966 notes/1968 publication;2025 precise comparison treatment','Valid with the actual inverse-system assumptions. Quasi-coherent individual values alone do not make arbitrary lim exact.',['Grothendieck1968','Liu2025'],'high',['R67-F07'])
C(75,[81,82],7,[60],[[1962,2013]],'tilde X→e couvrant; lim ind Hom','The representable sheaf tildeX covers the final sheaf of the stratification topos, its Čech powers are filtered colimits of representables of nilpotent diagonal neighbourhoods, and the Čech spectral sequence computes cohomology.','proof sketched; continues on7/061','A finite family of infinitesimally equal maps from a nilpotent thickening factors through some finite diagonal neighbourhood. This gives the filtered representable presentation; it does not make the formal completion an object of the original site.','A','Grothendieck1968 §5.1. The missing limit exactness details are exactly the continuation7/061 checked by the parent curator.','1966 draft/1968 publication','Established sheaf-level construction with the continuation’s compatibility assumptions. Package truncation is not an archive lacuna.',['Grothendieck1968','Liu2025'],'high',['R67-F07'])
